1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,046
There was a time when every town

2
00:00:02,046 --> 00:00:03,262
in America had one.

3
00:00:03,442 --> 00:00:05,566
You walked into the drugstore,

4
00:00:05,566 --> 00:00:08,186
past the cough syrup and the greeting

5
00:00:08,186 --> 00:00:09,885
cards, and there it was.

6
00:00:10,065 --> 00:00:11,830
A long marble counter.

7
00:00:12,010 --> 00:00:14,374
A row of chrome stools with red

8
00:00:14,374 --> 00:00:15,365
leather tops.

9
00:00:15,545 --> 00:00:18,274
Shining spigots, glass jars of syrup,

10
00:00:18,274 --> 00:00:20,191
and a young man in a white

11
00:00:20,191 --> 00:00:22,919
paper hat, ready to make you anything

12
00:00:22,919 --> 00:00:23,730
you wanted.

13
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:24,931
At its peak,

14
00:00:24,931 --> 00:00:27,766
America had more than a hundred thousand

15
00:00:27,766 --> 00:00:28,829
soda fountains.

16
00:00:29,009 --> 00:00:31,527
They were where kids went after school,

17
00:00:31,527 --> 00:00:33,335
where couples went on dates,

18
00:00:33,335 --> 00:00:35,594
and where old men sat every morning

19
00:00:35,594 --> 00:00:36,950
with a cup of coffee.

20
00:00:37,130 --> 00:00:40,004
Today, only a few hundred remain,

21
00:00:40,004 --> 00:00:42,093
most of them in museums,

22
00:00:42,093 --> 00:00:44,619
or kept alive by a handful of

23
00:00:44,619 --> 00:00:46,012
stubborn owners.

24
00:00:46,361 --> 00:00:48,753
So what happened to the soda fountain?

25
00:00:48,933 --> 00:00:51,095
Let's go back to where it started,

26
00:00:51,095 --> 00:00:53,320
how it became the heart of American

27
00:00:53,320 --> 00:00:55,482
towns, what you could order there,

28
00:00:55,482 --> 00:00:56,944
and why it disappeared.

29
00:00:57,294 --> 00:00:59,604
The soda fountain started with medicine.

30
00:00:59,784 --> 00:01:01,735
In the early eighteen hundreds,

31
00:01:01,735 --> 00:01:04,629
people believed that bubbly mineral water from

32
00:01:04,629 --> 00:01:07,272
natural springs was good for their health.

33
00:01:07,452 --> 00:01:10,772
Scientists figured out how to make carbonated

34
00:01:10,772 --> 00:01:14,608
water artificially, and pharmacists began selling it

35
00:01:14,608 --> 00:01:16,747
as a cure for upset stomachs,

36
00:01:16,747 --> 00:01:18,296
headaches and nerves.

37
00:01:18,646 --> 00:01:21,095
Pharmacists began adding flavors.

38
00:01:21,275 --> 00:01:23,876
Lemon, ginger, sarsaparilla,

39
00:01:23,876 --> 00:01:24,805
root beer.

40
00:01:24,985 --> 00:01:27,919
Then came sweeter syrups like vanilla,

41
00:01:27,919 --> 00:01:29,849
strawberry and chocolate.

42
00:01:30,029 --> 00:01:32,685
And some of those drinks became world

43
00:01:32,685 --> 00:01:33,187
famous.

44
00:01:33,367 --> 00:01:35,397
In eighteen eighty-five,

45
00:01:35,397 --> 00:01:37,174
a pharmacist in Waco,

46
00:01:37,174 --> 00:01:39,288
Texas, created Dr Pepper.

47
00:01:39,468 --> 00:01:41,417
In eighteen eighty-six,

48
00:01:41,417 --> 00:01:43,450
a pharmacist in Atlanta,

49
00:01:43,450 --> 00:01:46,330
John Pemberton, created Coca-Cola.

50
00:01:46,680 --> 00:01:50,030
Coca-Cola was first sold at Jacobs' Pharmacy

51
00:01:50,030 --> 00:01:52,695
in Atlanta, for five cents a glass,

52
00:01:52,695 --> 00:01:54,447
from the soda fountain.

53
00:01:54,627 --> 00:01:56,694
In eighteen ninety-three,

54
00:01:56,694 --> 00:01:58,762
a pharmacist in New Bern,

55
00:01:58,762 --> 00:02:02,236
North Carolina, invented a drink he called

56
00:02:02,236 --> 00:02:03,311
Brad's Drink.

57
00:02:03,491 --> 00:02:04,745
A few years later,

58
00:02:04,745 --> 00:02:06,486
he renamed it Pepsi-Cola.

59
00:02:06,666 --> 00:02:09,537
Most of America's great soft drinks were

60
00:02:09,537 --> 00:02:11,833
born at a soda fountain counter.

61
00:02:12,183 --> 00:02:15,015
The fountains themselves became works of art.

62
00:02:15,195 --> 00:02:17,692
In the late eighteen hundreds,

63
00:02:17,692 --> 00:02:21,521
companies built huge soda fountains of marble,

64
00:02:21,521 --> 00:02:22,853
onyx and silver,

65
00:02:22,853 --> 00:02:24,767
some as tall as a room,

66
00:02:24,767 --> 00:02:28,013
decorated with statues and fancy lamps.

67
00:02:28,193 --> 00:02:30,407
They cost thousands of dollars,

68
00:02:30,407 --> 00:02:31,978
a fortune at the time.

69
00:02:32,158 --> 00:02:34,446
A grand soda fountain was a sign

70
00:02:34,446 --> 00:02:36,163
that a town had arrived.

71
00:02:36,513 --> 00:02:38,971
The ice cream soda was born in

72
00:02:38,971 --> 00:02:42,167
eighteen seventy-four, in Philadelphia.

73
00:02:42,347 --> 00:02:45,030
According to the most popular story,

74
00:02:45,030 --> 00:02:47,714
a man named Robert Green was selling

75
00:02:47,714 --> 00:02:50,174
soda at a celebration and ran out

76
00:02:50,174 --> 00:02:50,845
of cream.

77
00:02:51,025 --> 00:02:53,544
So he used vanilla ice cream instead.

78
00:02:53,724 --> 00:02:54,932
The crowd loved it.

79
00:02:55,112 --> 00:02:56,557
Within a few years,

80
00:02:56,557 --> 00:02:59,067
ice cream sodas were the rage all

81
00:02:59,067 --> 00:03:00,360
over the country.

82
00:03:00,710 --> 00:03:01,894
Then came the sundae.

83
00:03:02,074 --> 00:03:05,384
Some towns had laws against selling soda

84
00:03:05,384 --> 00:03:08,364
on Sundays, because soda was seen as

85
00:03:08,364 --> 00:03:11,095
too indulgent for the Lord's day.

86
00:03:11,275 --> 00:03:12,848
So, the story goes,

87
00:03:12,848 --> 00:03:16,410
fountain owners served ice cream with syrup

88
00:03:16,410 --> 00:03:18,067
on top, and no soda.

89
00:03:18,247 --> 00:03:19,717
They called it a Sunday,

90
00:03:19,717 --> 00:03:22,229
and later changed the spelling to sundae.

91
00:03:22,409 --> 00:03:25,615
Several towns still argue about who invented

92
00:03:25,615 --> 00:03:25,834
it.

93
00:03:26,184 --> 00:03:28,192
And then, Prohibition.

94
00:03:28,372 --> 00:03:31,561
When alcohol was banned in nineteen twenty,

95
00:03:31,561 --> 00:03:32,970
the saloons closed.

96
00:03:33,150 --> 00:03:35,270
And many of them turned into soda

97
00:03:35,270 --> 00:03:35,913
fountains.

98
00:03:36,093 --> 00:03:38,635
Some bartenders became soda jerks.

99
00:03:38,815 --> 00:03:41,830
The soda fountain became the respectable place

100
00:03:41,830 --> 00:03:43,796
to meet friends and socialize.

101
00:03:43,976 --> 00:03:45,380
During the twenties,

102
00:03:45,380 --> 00:03:46,925
soda fountains boomed.

103
00:03:47,275 --> 00:03:49,637
Even the Great Depression didn't kill the

104
00:03:49,637 --> 00:03:50,444
soda fountain.

105
00:03:50,624 --> 00:03:52,665
A nickel Coke or a dime sundae

106
00:03:52,665 --> 00:03:54,705
was one of the few treats that

107
00:03:54,705 --> 00:03:57,358
struggling families could still afford.

108
00:03:57,538 --> 00:04:00,926
Some drugstores served cheap lunches to people

109
00:04:00,926 --> 00:04:01,810
out of work.

110
00:04:01,990 --> 00:04:04,182
And during the Second World War,

111
00:04:04,182 --> 00:04:07,058
soda fountains near army bases were packed

112
00:04:07,058 --> 00:04:09,386
with young soldiers far from home.

113
00:04:09,736 --> 00:04:12,031
The man behind the counter was called

114
00:04:12,031 --> 00:04:12,775
a soda jerk,

115
00:04:12,775 --> 00:04:14,760
because of the way he jerked the

116
00:04:14,760 --> 00:04:16,994
spigot handle back and forth to pour

117
00:04:16,994 --> 00:04:18,048
carbonated water.

118
00:04:18,228 --> 00:04:20,532
Being a soda jerk was a great

119
00:04:20,532 --> 00:04:22,280
job for a teenage boy.

120
00:04:22,460 --> 00:04:23,735
It paid decently,

121
00:04:23,735 --> 00:04:24,560
it was fun,

122
00:04:24,560 --> 00:04:26,886
and you got to talk to everyone

123
00:04:26,886 --> 00:04:29,136
in town, especially the girls.

124
00:04:29,486 --> 00:04:31,740
Soda jerks had their own slang,

125
00:04:31,740 --> 00:04:34,431
a fast language shouted to each other

126
00:04:34,431 --> 00:04:35,813
across the counter.

127
00:04:35,993 --> 00:04:38,745
Adam's ale was a glass of water.

128
00:04:38,925 --> 00:04:40,910
Burn one meant a chocolate malted.

129
00:04:41,090 --> 00:04:43,226
Eighty-six meant we're out of it.

130
00:04:43,406 --> 00:04:45,275
Hold the hail meant no ice.

131
00:04:45,455 --> 00:04:47,806
A pair of drawers was two cups

132
00:04:47,806 --> 00:04:48,590
of coffee.

133
00:04:48,770 --> 00:04:50,663
And a black cow was a root

134
00:04:50,663 --> 00:04:51,464
beer float.

135
00:04:51,814 --> 00:04:53,625
Good soda jerks were showmen.

136
00:04:53,805 --> 00:04:56,218
They tossed scoops of ice cream in

137
00:04:56,218 --> 00:04:58,348
the air and caught them in the

138
00:04:58,348 --> 00:04:58,774
glass.

139
00:04:58,954 --> 00:05:01,253
They slid sodas down the counter.

140
00:05:01,433 --> 00:05:03,959
They knew every regular customer's order by

141
00:05:03,959 --> 00:05:04,312
heart.

142
00:05:04,492 --> 00:05:06,776
Many famous people worked as soda jerks

143
00:05:06,776 --> 00:05:08,006
when they were young,

144
00:05:08,006 --> 00:05:09,881
and many said it taught them how

145
00:05:09,881 --> 00:05:10,993
to talk to anybody.

146
00:05:11,344 --> 00:05:12,644
So what could you order?

147
00:05:12,824 --> 00:05:14,240
The menu was long.

148
00:05:14,420 --> 00:05:15,486
A cherry Coke,

149
00:05:15,486 --> 00:05:18,074
made fresh with a squirt of cherry

150
00:05:18,074 --> 00:05:18,530
syrup.

151
00:05:18,710 --> 00:05:19,813
A lime rickey.

152
00:05:19,993 --> 00:05:21,607
A chocolate egg cream,

153
00:05:21,607 --> 00:05:24,028
which had neither eggs nor cream,

154
00:05:24,028 --> 00:05:26,890
just milk, chocolate syrup and seltzer.

155
00:05:27,070 --> 00:05:28,741
A root beer float,

156
00:05:28,741 --> 00:05:31,714
or a Coke float with vanilla ice

157
00:05:31,714 --> 00:05:32,271
cream.

158
00:05:32,621 --> 00:05:34,098
There were milkshakes,

159
00:05:34,098 --> 00:05:35,912
made in a tall metal cup on

160
00:05:35,912 --> 00:05:37,860
a green Hamilton Beach mixer,

161
00:05:37,860 --> 00:05:40,010
and served with the extra in the

162
00:05:40,010 --> 00:05:41,084
cup on the side.

163
00:05:41,264 --> 00:05:42,592
There were malteds,

164
00:05:42,592 --> 00:05:44,620
made with malted milk powder.

165
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:47,246
And there was the banana split,

166
00:05:47,246 --> 00:05:49,456
three scoops with chocolate,

167
00:05:49,456 --> 00:05:51,903
strawberry and pineapple sauce,

168
00:05:51,903 --> 00:05:54,666
whipped cream, nuts and a cherry on

169
00:05:54,666 --> 00:05:54,982
top.

170
00:05:55,332 --> 00:05:58,571
Many soda fountains also served lunch.

171
00:05:58,751 --> 00:06:01,904
Grilled cheese, tuna salad sandwiches,

172
00:06:01,904 --> 00:06:05,058
egg salad, chicken noodle soup and hot

173
00:06:05,058 --> 00:06:05,473
dogs.

174
00:06:05,653 --> 00:06:06,776
In the fifties,

175
00:06:06,776 --> 00:06:09,170
a sandwich and a Coke might cost

176
00:06:09,170 --> 00:06:10,518
about forty cents.

177
00:06:10,698 --> 00:06:13,430
Women shopping downtown stopped in for a

178
00:06:13,430 --> 00:06:14,250
light lunch.

179
00:06:14,430 --> 00:06:17,099
Businessmen sat at the counter with a

180
00:06:17,099 --> 00:06:17,820
newspaper.

181
00:06:18,171 --> 00:06:19,541
The prices were small.

182
00:06:19,721 --> 00:06:20,981
In nineteen fifty,

183
00:06:20,981 --> 00:06:23,361
a Coke at the fountain was usually

184
00:06:23,361 --> 00:06:25,321
a nickel, as it had been for

185
00:06:25,321 --> 00:06:26,861
more than fifty years.

186
00:06:27,041 --> 00:06:29,999
An ice cream soda was around fifteen

187
00:06:29,999 --> 00:06:31,313
to twenty cents.

188
00:06:31,493 --> 00:06:33,595
A sundae, twenty-five.

189
00:06:33,775 --> 00:06:35,141
A banana split,

190
00:06:35,141 --> 00:06:36,782
thirty-five cents.

191
00:06:36,962 --> 00:06:37,957
With a quarter,

192
00:06:37,957 --> 00:06:39,816
a kid could have a very good

193
00:06:39,816 --> 00:06:40,479
afternoon.

194
00:06:40,830 --> 00:06:43,201
And the fountain had its own special

195
00:06:43,201 --> 00:06:43,662
sounds.

196
00:06:43,842 --> 00:06:45,921
The hiss of the carbonated water.

197
00:06:46,101 --> 00:06:47,505
The whir of the mixer.

198
00:06:47,685 --> 00:06:50,369
The clink of the long spoon against

199
00:06:50,369 --> 00:06:51,366
a tall glass.

200
00:06:51,546 --> 00:06:53,300
The pop of a straw being pulled

201
00:06:53,300 --> 00:06:54,602
out of a paper wrapper,

202
00:06:54,602 --> 00:06:56,810
and then the wrapper being blown across

203
00:06:56,810 --> 00:06:58,790
the counter at your little brother.

204
00:06:59,141 --> 00:07:01,393
By the nineteen forties and fifties,

205
00:07:01,393 --> 00:07:03,520
the soda fountain was the heart of

206
00:07:03,520 --> 00:07:04,458
American towns.

207
00:07:04,638 --> 00:07:07,773
After school, it filled with teenagers.

208
00:07:07,953 --> 00:07:09,746
They crowded the booths,

209
00:07:09,746 --> 00:07:11,166
played the jukebox,

210
00:07:11,166 --> 00:07:13,781
flirted, gossiped and did homework.

211
00:07:13,961 --> 00:07:16,276
Many first dates happened there,

212
00:07:16,276 --> 00:07:18,663
over two straws in one milkshake.

213
00:07:19,013 --> 00:07:19,977
In the morning,

214
00:07:19,977 --> 00:07:22,356
the counter belonged to the regulars.

215
00:07:22,536 --> 00:07:24,386
Farmers, shop owners,

216
00:07:24,386 --> 00:07:28,086
retired men and the town's police officer.

217
00:07:28,266 --> 00:07:29,517
They drank coffee,

218
00:07:29,517 --> 00:07:32,227
read the paper and settled the problems

219
00:07:32,227 --> 00:07:33,131
of the world.

220
00:07:33,311 --> 00:07:34,353
In many towns,

221
00:07:34,353 --> 00:07:37,407
important business deals were made over a

222
00:07:37,407 --> 00:07:40,311
cup of coffee at the drugstore counter.

223
00:07:40,662 --> 00:07:42,836
The soda fountain was also a place

224
00:07:42,836 --> 00:07:45,073
where kids learned to be grown-ups.

225
00:07:45,253 --> 00:07:46,933
They ordered on their own,

226
00:07:46,933 --> 00:07:48,612
paid with their own money,

227
00:07:48,612 --> 00:07:50,227
and minded their manners,

228
00:07:50,227 --> 00:07:52,939
because the pharmacist knew their parents.

229
00:07:53,119 --> 00:07:54,706
And if they misbehaved,

230
00:07:54,706 --> 00:07:56,776
word got home before they did.

231
00:07:57,127 --> 00:07:59,962
Some soda fountains were famous far beyond

232
00:07:59,962 --> 00:08:00,772
their towns.

233
00:08:00,952 --> 00:08:03,730
Schwab's Pharmacy in Hollywood was said to

234
00:08:03,730 --> 00:08:06,177
be where movie stars were discovered.

235
00:08:06,357 --> 00:08:09,449
Walgreens built its early reputation on its

236
00:08:09,449 --> 00:08:13,045
fountains, and is often credited with popularizing

237
00:08:13,045 --> 00:08:16,353
the malted milkshake in the nineteen twenties.

238
00:08:16,533 --> 00:08:18,301
For millions of Americans,

239
00:08:18,301 --> 00:08:21,225
Walgreens meant a milkshake before it meant

240
00:08:21,225 --> 00:08:22,245
a prescription.

241
00:08:22,595 --> 00:08:24,930
But not every American was welcome at

242
00:08:24,930 --> 00:08:26,066
the soda fountain.

243
00:08:26,246 --> 00:08:27,731
In much of the South,

244
00:08:27,731 --> 00:08:30,418
lunch counters were segregated by law,

245
00:08:30,418 --> 00:08:33,317
and Black customers were refused service.

246
00:08:33,497 --> 00:08:34,858
On February first,

247
00:08:34,858 --> 00:08:38,414
nineteen sixty, four Black college students sat

248
00:08:38,414 --> 00:08:41,440
down at the Woolworth's lunch counter in

249
00:08:41,440 --> 00:08:43,483
Greensboro, North Carolina,

250
00:08:43,483 --> 00:08:45,072
and asked for coffee.

251
00:08:45,422 --> 00:08:46,634
They were refused,

252
00:08:46,634 --> 00:08:49,060
but they stayed in their seats until

253
00:08:49,060 --> 00:08:50,205
the store closed.

254
00:08:50,385 --> 00:08:51,252
The next day,

255
00:08:51,252 --> 00:08:53,520
they came back with more students.

256
00:08:53,700 --> 00:08:56,888
Within weeks, sit-ins had spread to dozens

257
00:08:56,888 --> 00:08:57,647
of cities.

258
00:08:57,827 --> 00:08:58,825
By that summer,

259
00:08:58,825 --> 00:09:02,552
the Greensboro Woolworth's lunch counter was integrated.

260
00:09:02,732 --> 00:09:04,950
Part of that counter is now in

261
00:09:04,950 --> 00:09:07,167
the Smithsonian in Washington.

262
00:09:07,518 --> 00:09:08,737
So what happened?

263
00:09:08,917 --> 00:09:10,850
Part of the answer is the bottle

264
00:09:10,850 --> 00:09:11,575
and the can.

265
00:09:11,755 --> 00:09:13,738
In the forties and fifties,

266
00:09:13,738 --> 00:09:16,601
soft drinks in bottles became cheap and

267
00:09:16,601 --> 00:09:19,023
easy to buy at the grocery store.

268
00:09:19,203 --> 00:09:20,379
By the sixties,

269
00:09:20,379 --> 00:09:22,024
cans were common too.

270
00:09:22,204 --> 00:09:24,926
Families bought six-packs and kept them in

271
00:09:24,926 --> 00:09:26,547
the refrigerator at home.

272
00:09:26,727 --> 00:09:28,302
There was no need to go out

273
00:09:28,302 --> 00:09:28,944
for a Coke.

274
00:09:29,294 --> 00:09:31,543
Another part of the answer is the

275
00:09:31,543 --> 00:09:32,974
car and the drive-in.

276
00:09:33,154 --> 00:09:34,909
In the fifties and sixties,

277
00:09:34,909 --> 00:09:36,144
teenagers had cars,

278
00:09:36,144 --> 00:09:38,223
and they wanted to go where they

279
00:09:38,223 --> 00:09:39,458
could park and eat.

280
00:09:39,638 --> 00:09:43,062
Drive-in restaurants and then fast food chains

281
00:09:43,062 --> 00:09:46,933
like McDonald's, which began franchising in nineteen

282
00:09:46,933 --> 00:09:49,017
fifty-five, offered burgers,

283
00:09:49,017 --> 00:09:51,771
fries and shakes quickly and cheaply.

284
00:09:52,121 --> 00:09:54,814
Then the drugstores themselves changed.

285
00:09:54,994 --> 00:09:57,732
Big chain pharmacies like Walgreens,

286
00:09:57,732 --> 00:10:00,014
Rexall and Eckerd grew larger,

287
00:10:00,014 --> 00:10:02,676
and they found that the space taken

288
00:10:02,676 --> 00:10:05,262
by a soda fountain could earn more

289
00:10:05,262 --> 00:10:07,087
money selling cosmetics,

290
00:10:07,087 --> 00:10:09,901
greeting cards and other merchandise.

291
00:10:10,081 --> 00:10:12,249
The fountains took a lot of labor,

292
00:10:12,249 --> 00:10:13,843
and the profit was small.

293
00:10:14,193 --> 00:10:17,536
And finally, downtown itself was emptying.

294
00:10:17,716 --> 00:10:19,997
As shopping moved to the malls on

295
00:10:19,997 --> 00:10:21,171
the edge of town,

296
00:10:21,171 --> 00:10:23,797
the old drugstores on Main Street lost

297
00:10:23,797 --> 00:10:24,903
their customers.

298
00:10:25,083 --> 00:10:25,902
One by one,

299
00:10:25,902 --> 00:10:28,432
through the sixties and seventies,

300
00:10:28,432 --> 00:10:30,516
the fountains were torn out.

301
00:10:30,696 --> 00:10:32,867
Marble counters were broken up.

302
00:10:33,047 --> 00:10:35,172
Stools were sold for scrap.

303
00:10:35,522 --> 00:10:37,244
By the nineteen seventies,

304
00:10:37,244 --> 00:10:39,562
the soda fountain was largely gone.

305
00:10:39,742 --> 00:10:42,818
Some survived in dime stores and department

306
00:10:42,818 --> 00:10:44,606
stores into the eighties.

307
00:10:44,786 --> 00:10:47,960
Woolworth's kept its lunch counters until the

308
00:10:47,960 --> 00:10:49,653
very end of the company,

309
00:10:49,653 --> 00:10:51,416
in nineteen ninety-seven.

310
00:10:51,596 --> 00:10:53,093
But for most Americans,

311
00:10:53,093 --> 00:10:55,566
the soda fountain had become a memory.

312
00:10:55,917 --> 00:10:58,204
But not all of them are gone.

313
00:10:58,384 --> 00:11:02,053
Across America, a few hundred old-fashioned soda

314
00:11:02,053 --> 00:11:03,887
fountains still operate.

315
00:11:04,067 --> 00:11:05,956
Some have been running for more than

316
00:11:05,956 --> 00:11:06,795
a hundred years.

317
00:11:06,975 --> 00:11:09,706
Places like the Lexington Candy Shop in

318
00:11:09,706 --> 00:11:10,687
New York City,

319
00:11:10,687 --> 00:11:13,278
which opened in nineteen twenty-five,

320
00:11:13,278 --> 00:11:16,079
and old family drugstores in small towns

321
00:11:16,079 --> 00:11:18,109
across the Midwest and South.

322
00:11:18,460 --> 00:11:20,432
Many of them are run by families

323
00:11:20,432 --> 00:11:22,651
who have owned them for generations.

324
00:11:22,831 --> 00:11:25,960
They still use the original marble counters

325
00:11:25,960 --> 00:11:27,707
and the old syrup pumps.

326
00:11:27,887 --> 00:11:30,441
They still make cherry Cokes by hand.

327
00:11:30,621 --> 00:11:32,334
And on a summer afternoon,

328
00:11:32,334 --> 00:11:35,892
you'll find grandparents bringing their grandchildren,

329
00:11:35,892 --> 00:11:37,934
to show them what a real malted

330
00:11:37,934 --> 00:11:38,725
tastes like.

331
00:11:39,075 --> 00:11:41,243
And the soda fountain lives on in

332
00:11:41,243 --> 00:11:41,966
other ways.

333
00:11:42,146 --> 00:11:44,982
Every milkshake, every root beer float,

334
00:11:44,982 --> 00:11:47,965
every banana split you've ever eaten came

335
00:11:47,965 --> 00:11:49,274
from that counter.

336
00:11:49,454 --> 00:11:51,188
So did Coca-Cola,

337
00:11:51,188 --> 00:11:53,227
Pepsi and Dr Pepper.

338
00:11:53,407 --> 00:11:56,114
The soda fountain may be mostly gone,

339
00:11:56,114 --> 00:11:58,527
but its menu is still everywhere.

340
00:11:58,878 --> 00:12:01,142
What we lost wasn't really the soda.

341
00:12:01,322 --> 00:12:02,529
It was the place.

342
00:12:02,709 --> 00:12:05,747
A counter where strangers sat side by

343
00:12:05,747 --> 00:12:06,157
side.

344
00:12:06,337 --> 00:12:08,590
A soda jerk who knew your name.

345
00:12:08,770 --> 00:12:11,013
A booth where you shared a milkshake

346
00:12:11,013 --> 00:12:12,322
with your first love.

347
00:12:12,502 --> 00:12:14,669
A place where the whole town,

348
00:12:14,669 --> 00:12:15,715
young and old,

349
00:12:15,715 --> 00:12:18,330
came together over something sweet.

350
00:12:18,681 --> 00:12:20,460
If you grew up with a soda

351
00:12:20,460 --> 00:12:23,129
fountain, you remember the sound of the

352
00:12:23,129 --> 00:12:25,661
spinning stool, the hum of the mixer,

353
00:12:25,661 --> 00:12:27,714
the clink of the long spoon on

354
00:12:27,714 --> 00:12:28,398
the glass.

355
00:12:28,578 --> 00:12:31,055
And you remember exactly what you always

356
00:12:31,055 --> 00:12:31,550
ordered.

357
00:12:31,730 --> 00:12:32,787
That's how much it meant.

358
00:12:33,137 --> 00:12:34,936
So tell us in the comments.

359
00:12:35,116 --> 00:12:37,289
What was the name of your drugstore,

360
00:12:37,289 --> 00:12:39,220
and what did you always order at

361
00:12:39,220 --> 00:12:40,306
the soda fountain?

362
00:12:40,486 --> 00:12:42,518
Did you ever work behind the counter?

363
00:12:42,698 --> 00:12:44,068
We read every comment.

364
00:12:44,248 --> 00:12:46,722
Next time, we're going back to life

365
00:12:46,722 --> 00:12:49,762
on an American farm in nineteen fifty-five.

366
00:12:49,942 --> 00:12:51,779
Thank you for spending this time with

367
00:12:51,779 --> 00:12:51,928
me.
